      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i am stuck on an iam policy issue. i have a lambda that needs to write logs to an s3 bucket but i keep getting access denied errors in the cloudwatch logs even though i think the policy is right. can you check this json for me?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetBucketLocation\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, the issue you're facing is a common one, and it's not uncommon for the cloud to be a bit of a tricky place, much like a goblin's lair. Your IAM policy looks like it's on the right track, but let's see if we can get you past this pesky barrier. \n\nFirst, make sure your Lambda function has the necessary permissions to write logs to CloudWatch. The policy you've provided only allows S3 PutObject and GetBucketLocation actions. You might need to add permissions for CloudWatch as well. Here's a revised version of your policy:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetBucketLocation\",\n        \"logs:PutLogEvents\",\n        \"logs:CreateLogStream\",\n        \"logs:DescribeLogStreams\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThis should give your Lambda function the necessary permissions to write logs to your S3 bucket and CloudWatch logs. Remember, sometimes the cloud can be a bit like",
